Yeah. One bit is a choice. A sequence of bits can be a sentence.

Take a light that can only be on or off. Give it a little time and you can send this:

Blink blink blink. Blaaank blaaank blaaank. Blink blink blink.

That’s SOS in Morse code. The light still has two states. Duration makes dots and dashes, pauses tell you what belongs together, and a shared code lets someone across the water read it. Even the off part is doing work.

Same with montage. Show his face. Cut to the empty chair. Hold. Cut back. At any instant you’re looking at one shot. By the time you return to his face, the chair has changed how you read it.

Sometimes when a choice looks too binary, there’s another question worth asking. What can I express by arranging these choices over time?